  "reasoning": "The patient presents with severe stomach pain, constipation, headaches, and nightly chills. This constellation of symptoms strongly aligns with typhoid fever, which characteristically causes abdominal pain, gastrointestinal disturbances (frequently constipation in the early stages), systemic symptoms like chills/fever, and prominent headaches. Reference [1] directly maps a nearly identical symptom set (stomach pain, bloating, constipation, high fever, and chills) to typhoid. Other options like peptic ulcer disease or gastroesophageal reflux disease lack the systemic chills/headache component, while malaria typically lacks prominent constipation and localized severe stomach pain as primary presenting features. Therefore, typhoid is the most accurate diagnosis.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]typhoid[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}

